{"page": 2, "text": "Hilton Grand Vacations Reports Third Quarter 2026 Results ORLANDO, Fla. (Oct. 29, 2026) \u2014 Hilton Grand Vacations Inc. (NYSE: HGV) (\u201cHGV\u201d or \u201cthe Company\u201d) today reports its third quarter 2026 results. Third Quarter 2026 Results1 \u2022Total contract sales were $807 million, a decrease of 11.0% compared to the third quarter of 2025. \u2022Total revenues were $1.961 billion. \u25e6Total revenues were affected by a net construction recognition of $536 million. \u2022Net income attributable to stockholders was $291 million and diluted EPS was $3.70. \u25e6Adjusted net income attributable to stockholders was $317 million and adjusted diluted EPS was $4.03. \u25e6Net income and Adjusted net income attributable to stockholders were affected by a net construction recognition of $296 million, or $3.76 per share. \u2022Adjusted EBITDA attributable to stockholders was $609 million. \u25e6Adjusted EBITDA attributable to stockholders was affected by a net construction recognition of $296 million. \u2022The provision for financing receivable losses was 12.5% of owned contract sales primarily driven by the changes to the allowance for financing receivables losses methodology implemented during the quarter. \u2022The Company announces the promotion of Mike Riley to EVP, chief sales & marketing officer. \u2022During the third quarter, the Company repurchased 2.4 million shares of common stock for $110 million. \u25e6As of Oct. 22, 2026, the Company has $617 million of remaining availability under the share repurchase plans. \u2022The Company is reducing its guidance for the full year 2026 Adjusted EBITDA, excluding deferrals and recognitions, to a range of $1.165 billion to $1.180 billion, from the prior range of $1.225 billion to $1.265 billion. \u201cThe underlying business model, recurring revenue streams, and cash flow generation of our business remained resilient in the third quarter, but our sales results fell short of our expectations as the initiatives we put in place over the summer to improve execution are taking longer to ramp than we anticipated,\u201d said Mark Wang, CEO of Hilton Grand Vacations. \u201cWhile we are not satisfied with our third quarter results, we were encouraged to see initial signs of progress as we exited the quarter, and we\u2019ve accelerated our implementation efforts while adding new measures to drive conversion.\u201d \u201cLooking ahead, we are updating our outlook to reflect our year-to-date performance and a more realistic view on the timing and ramp of our initiatives,\u201d Wang continued. \u201cOur near-term priorities are clear: enhance sales execution by increasing our conversion rates and return the business to consistent and profitable growth. We remain confident in our business model, the quality of our brands and service, and our product and inventory position, including the recent opening of Ka Haku, a Hilton Club in Waikiki.\u201d 1.The Company\u2019s current period results and prior year results include impacts related to deferrals of revenues and direct expenses related to the Sales of Vacation Ownership Intervals or\n"}
{"page": 3, "text": "Vacation Ownership Interests (\u201cVOIs\u201d) under construction that are recognized when construction is complete. These impacts are reflected in the sub-bullets. 1\n"}
{"page": 4, "text": "Overview For the quarter ended Sept. 30, 2026, diluted EPS was $3.70 compared to $0.28 for the quarter ended Sept. 30, 2025. Net income attributable to stockholders and Adjusted EBITDA attributable to stockholders were $291 million and $609 million, for the quarter ended Sept. 30, 2026, compared to Net income attributable to stockholders and Adjusted EBITDA attributable to stockholders of $25 million and $245 million, for the quarter ended Sept. 30, 2025. Total revenues for the quarter ended Sept. 30, 2026, were $1.961 billion compared to $1.300 billion for the quarter ended Sept. 30, 2025. Net income attributable to stockholders and Adjusted EBITDA attributable to stockholders for the quarter ended Sept. 30, 2026, included a net construction recognition of $296 million relating to a project in Hawaii. Net income attributable to stockholders and Adjusted EBITDA attributable to stockholders for the quarter ended Sept. 30, 2025, included a net construction deferral of $57 million relating to projects under construction in Hawaii and Japan. Consolidated Segment Highlights \u2014 Third Quarter of 2026 Real Estate Sales and Financing For the quarter ended Sept. 30, 2026, Real Estate Sales and Financing segment revenues were $1.418 billion, an increase of $629 million compared to the quarter ended Sept. 30, 2025. Real Estate Sales and Financing segment Adjusted EBITDA and Adjusted EBITDA profit margin were $557 million and 39.3%, for the quarter ended Sept. 30, 2026, compared to $184 million and 23.3%, for the quarter ended Sept. 30, 2025. Real Estate Sales and Financing segment revenues in the third quarter of 2026 increased due to a $651 million increase in Sales of VOI, net, and a $21 million increase in financing revenue partially offset by a $43 million decrease in fee-for-service commissions, package sales and other fees. Real Estate Sales and Financing segment Adjusted EBITDA reflects a net construction recognition of $296 million for the quarter ended Sept. 30, 2026, compared to $57 million net construction deferral for the quarter ended Sept. 30, 2025. The net construction recognition in 2026 increased reported Adjusted EBITDA attributable to stockholders, while the net construction deferral in 2025 decreased reported Adjusted EBITDA attributable to stockholders. Contract sales for the quarter ended Sept. 30, 2026, decreased $100 million to $807 million compared to the quarter ended Sept. 30, 2025. For the quarter ended Sept. 30, 2026, VPG decreased by 12.5% and tour flow increased 1.7% compared to the quarter ended Sept. 30, 2025. For the quarter ended Sept. 30, 2026, fee-for-service contract sales represented 11.5% of contract sales compared to 17.2% for the quarter ended Sept. 30, 2025. For the quarter ended Sept. 30, 2026, the provision for financing receivables losses decreased $37 million compared to the quarter ended Sept. 30, 2025, primarily due to the impact of the change in accounting estimate in connection with the methodology changes\n"}
{"page": 5, "text": "related to the allowance for originated financing receivable losses discussed below. Financing revenues for the quarter ended Sept. 30, 2026, increased by $21 million compared to the quarter ended Sept. 30, 2025. This was driven primarily by an increase in the average outstanding balance of the timeshare financing receivables portfolio and a decrease in the premium amortization of acquired timeshare financing receivables of $4 million. Resort Operations and Club Management For the quarter ended Sept. 30, 2026, Resort Operations and Club Management segment revenues were $416 million, an increase of $10 million compared to the quarter ended Sept. 30, 2025. Resort Operations and Club Management segment Adjusted EBITDA and Adjusted EBITDA profit margin were $158 million and 38.0%, for the quarter ended Sept. 30, 2026, compared to $159 million and 39.2%, for the quarter ended Sept. 30, 2025. Resort Operations and Club Management segment revenues increased primarily due to increases of $6 million in rental revenue and $3 million in resort and club management revenue. Balance Sheet and Liquidity Total cash and cash equivalents were $227 million and total restricted cash was $275 million as of Sept. 30, 2026. As of Sept. 30, 2026, the Company had $4.9 billion of corporate debt, net outstanding with a weighted average interest rate of 5.751% and $2.9 billion of non-recourse debt, net outstanding with a weighted average interest rate of 5.054%. As of Sept. 30, 2026, the Company\u2019s liquidity position consisted of $227 million of unrestricted cash and $457 million remaining borrowing capacity under the revolving facility. As of Sept. 30, 2026, the Company had $450 million remaining borrowing capacity under the Timeshare Facility and $175 million remaining borrowing capacity under the Big Cedar Timeshare Facility. As of Sept. 30, 2026, the Company had $1.3 billion of notes that were current on payments but not securitized. Of that figure, approximately $851 million could be monetized through either warehouse borrowing or securitization while another $383 million of mortgage notes anticipate being eligible following certain customary milestones such as first payment, deeding and recording. 2\n"}
{"page": 6, "text": "Free cash flow was $52 million for the quarter ended Sept. 30, 2026, compared to $(4) million for the same period in the prior year. Adjusted free cash flow was $63 million for the quarter ended Sept. 30, 2026, compared to $23 million for the same period in the prior year. Adjusted free cash flow for the quarter ended Sept. 30, 2026, and 2025 includes add-backs of $25 million and $49 million for acquisition and integration related costs. As of Sept. 30, 2026, the Company\u2019s total net leverage on a trailing 12-month basis was approximately 3.9x. Changes to the Company's Allowance for Financing Receivables Losses Methodology During the third quarter of 2026, the Company made certain changes to its allowance for financing receivables methodology to reflect the continued integration of its previously acquired businesses and the Company's expectation that future owners will represent a composite of all historical owners. The changes to the Company's methodology included combining the historical performance of timeshare financing receivables from these businesses into a single static pool model that stratifies the receivables by FICO score and equity percentage at the time of sale, consistent with the Company's integrated underwriting standards. These changes were accounted for prospectively as a change in accounting estimate. As a result, HGV recorded a $55 million decrease in the allowance for originated timeshare financing receivables. The change reduced the provision for financing receivables losses, which resulted in an increase to Sales of VOIs, net. 3\nTotal Construction Deferrals and/or Recognitions Included in Results Reported Under Accounting Standards Codification Topic 606 (\u201cASC 606\u201d) The Company\u2019s Adjusted EBITDA as reported under ASC 606 includes construction-related recognitions and deferrals of revenues and related expenses as detailed in Table T-1 below. Under ASC 606, the Company defers revenues and related expense